{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794391106,"nonce":"h23d5IIBlkcU4dRnSQyaRt3EVi8=","title":"GPU animation P2: a GPU node table, with transform and opacity deltas (the core change)","message":"Part of the GPU-deferral plan (umbrella issue). Depends on P0 (clock, uniform, slot lane) and P1 (descriptors).\n\n**Idea.** Emit primitives at their settled pose, once. An animated node then supplies a small delta that the GPU applies, so the cached batch stays valid for the whole animation. This is FLIP for everything: hit testing, accessibility and focus keep reading the settled layout position, which is what they read today (event_router ignores transforms and animation).\n\n**What replaces what.** Today the CPU evaluates each animated value every frame and patches primitive fields (`apply_binding_deltas` for motion bindings, `apply_css_deltas` for CSS animations and transitions), re-uploads the whole dynamic batch (`write_primitives_safe`; `write_primitives_partial` exists and has no callers), and bails to a full tree walk whenever text, SVG or an image sits in the animated subtree. CSS and motion layers that are promoted are blitted with CPU-computed uniforms. The node table replaces the patch and the re-upload.\n\n**Node table.** One row per animated node: parent slot, a delta (translate xy, scale xy about an origin, rotate z, opacity multiplier), flags. Static nodes use slot 0, the identity row, and pay nothing. Primitives carry `node_slot` (P0 decides the lane). The vertex stage composes the delta onto the rest pose in `sdf_core` (the existing vertex-stage AABB maths already uses centre, scale and a normalised 2x2). Opacity goes to the fragment stage as a flat varying, never a per-fragment fetch, because the A14 is fill-bound at about 2.5 ns per fragment (36c7812).\n\n**Two steps.**\n- P2a: the CPU writes evaluated rows each frame for animated nodes only (a small `write_buffer`), the shader applies them. This already removes the batch patch, the batch re-upload and the full-walk fallback for moving text.\n- P2b: rows hold descriptors and the vertex stage evaluates them from the frame clock. Per-frame CPU work for a running animation is then zero.\n\n**Tiers.** Tier 0 reads a storage buffer, tier 2 a data texture. Tier 1 (vertex-buffer instances) has no table read path: keep today's CPU patcher there as the fallback, selected by a capability flag, so no tier regresses. Open question: whether tier 1 can fetch from a vertex texture.\n\n**Hierarchy.** An animated ancestor moves its descendants. Each primitive stores its nearest animated ancestor's slot and the vertex stage walks the parent chain up to depth 4; deeper chains fall back to the CPU path.\n\n**Group opacity** (overlapping children) stays on offscreen layers, as now; the table value drives `LayerCompositeUniforms.opacity` and the promoted-layer blit uniforms, so one source feeds both.\n\n**Moving text.** Glyphs under an animated node need the slot too (`GpuGlyph`, and `GpuPrimitive` Text). The atlas bitmap is not re-rasterised, so scaling up resamples it, as it does today.\n\n**Verify while here.** The survey found `apply_binding_deltas` touches only bounds, corner_radius, rotation, local_affine and the alpha values, and saw clip, gradient_params, shadow offset and blur and border width left stale on that path. Unconfirmed. Also unconfirmed: how often real CSS animations land in the background batch rather than the dynamic or composite one, which decides how much `apply_css_deltas` is used.\n\n**Public API.** None for CSS or `motion()` users. blinc_gpu: `GpuPrimitive` gains a field (breaking for struct literals; use builders), new `NodeTable` and renderer entry points. blinc_layout: an animation-track registry replaces the composite-binding metadata. blinc_abi: its display-list decoder and `set_visual` need a slot path, tracked separately.\n\n**Constraints from the repo.** Windows FXC: a switch case ending in `discard` is rejected (the `no_shader_has_a_switch_case_fxc_rejects` test enforces it); `aux` is a reserved file name. PowerVR: no dynamic indexing of array literals (use `match`); `layer_composite.rs` still does `positions[vertex_index]`. Explicit-LOD sampling in non-uniform branches.\n\nAcceptance: old-versus-new pixel parity (the method used for the shader ports) on the existing motion and CSS animation scenes, on Metal and a software adapter; the P0 benchmark shows the per-frame patch, upload and full-walk costs gone; the three tiers still render; the Windows HLSL test passes.","files":null}]}